VARTOOLS — macOS Installation Notes
====================================

This file covers macOS-specific installation details.  The main ``README``
covers platform-independent material (dependencies, general ``configure``
options, ``pyvartools``, etc.); read it first.

VARTOOLS builds and runs on modern macOS (tested on Apple Silicon and Intel).
A known macOS-specific issue with unnamed POSIX semaphores is worked around
at compile time, so parallel processing via ``-parallel`` works correctly.

The instructions below assume `Homebrew <https://brew.sh>`_ as the package
manager.  Homebrew installs to different prefixes depending on the CPU:

* Intel Macs: ``/usr/local``
* Apple Silicon (M1/M2/M3/M4): ``/opt/homebrew``

The commands below use ``$(brew --prefix)`` to stay architecture-agnostic.


1. Prerequisites
----------------

1.1 Xcode Command Line Tools
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Apple's ``clang`` satisfies the C-compiler requirement.  Install the Command
Line Tools (bundled with Xcode, or installable standalone)::

    xcode-select --install

This provides ``clang``, ``make``, ``ld``, and the macOS system headers.

1.2 Homebrew packages
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

macOS's default ``libtool`` is Apple's library-archive tool — **not** the
GNU libtool that autotools expects.  Homebrew installs GNU libtool under
the name ``glibtool`` / ``glibtoolize`` to avoid the conflict.  This only
matters if you run ``./autogen.sh``; the pre-generated ``configure`` in
the release tarball works with Apple's tools.

macOS also does not ship a Fortran compiler, and the embedded Python and R
commands require their respective development libraries.  Install
everything required plus all optional dependencies::

    brew update
    brew install \
        gcc               `# brings gfortran` \
        libtool           `# GNU libtool, installed as glibtool` \
        autoconf automake `# only needed for ./autogen.sh` \
        cfitsio \
        gsl \
        nfft              `# pulls in fftw; enables -FTP NFFT-batched sums` \
        python@3          `# or use system Python 3` \
        numpy \
        r

``pthread`` is part of libSystem on macOS — no extra install is needed,
and ``configure``'s ``-lpthread`` check succeeds automatically.

1.3 CSPICE (not in Homebrew)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Build CSPICE from source.  Pick the matching distribution from
`NAIF's download page <https://naif.jpl.nasa.gov/pub/naif/toolkit/C/>`_:

* Intel Macs: ``PC_MacOSX_GCC_64bit``
* Apple Silicon: ``MacM1_OSX_clang_64bit``

For Apple Silicon::

    mkdir -p ~/src && cd ~/src
    curl -O https://naif.jpl.nasa.gov/pub/naif/toolkit//C/MacM1_OSX_clang_64bit/packages/cspice.tar.Z
    gunzip cspice.tar.Z
    tar -xf cspice.tar
    cd cspice
    sh makeall.csh       # ("csh" script but plain sh works fine)

This produces ``~/src/cspice/lib/cspice.a`` and ``~/src/cspice/include/``,
which you'll point ``configure`` at with ``--with-cspice=~/src/cspice``.


2. Build and install
--------------------

The key difference from Linux is that Homebrew-installed libraries are in
``$(brew --prefix)`` (i.e. ``/opt/homebrew`` on Apple Silicon, ``/usr/local``
on Intel), which is not always on the default compiler / linker search
path.  Pass the paths explicitly with ``CPPFLAGS`` / ``LDFLAGS``::

    HOMEBREW_PREFIX=$(brew --prefix)
    ./configure \
        --prefix=$HOME/.local \
        --with-cspice=$HOME/src/cspice \
        CPPFLAGS="-I${HOMEBREW_PREFIX}/include" \
        LDFLAGS="-L${HOMEBREW_PREFIX}/lib" \
        FC=gfortran F77=gfortran
    make
    make install

**FC / F77 note.**  Homebrew's ``gcc`` package provides ``gfortran``, but
the system's default ``F77`` environment variable may point at something
else (or be unset).  Always pass ``F77=gfortran FC=gfortran`` explicitly
on macOS unless you're sure of the autodetection.

**Apple Silicon note.**  If you see linker errors about incompatible
architectures (``building for macOS-x86_64 but attempting to link with
file built for macOS-arm64``), you probably have a mix of Intel and ARM
Homebrew installations.  ``brew doctor`` will flag this.

Verify the installation::

    $HOME/.local/bin/vartools -help


3. pyvartools (Python wrapper)
------------------------------

Use the same Python interpreter that ``configure`` detected at build time
(otherwise the ``-python`` command inside ``vartools`` won't be able to
``import`` the packages you install).  The safest approach is a conda or
virtual environment::

    cd python
    pip install -e .

See the main ``README`` for details.


4. Typical problems
-------------------

* **``configure`` can't find ``libcfitsio`` / ``libgsl``** — you're missing
  the ``CPPFLAGS`` / ``LDFLAGS`` overrides pointing at your Homebrew prefix.
  See the example ``configure`` invocation above.

* **``ld: library not found for -lgfortran``** — the system default Fortran
  library path isn't set.  With Homebrew's ``gcc``, ``gfortran`` lives in
  ``$(brew --prefix)/lib/gcc/current``.  Pass ``F77=gfortran FC=gfortran``
  and make sure ``LDFLAGS`` includes ``-L$(brew --prefix)/lib``.

* **``configure``'s ``autoreconf`` step fails with ``libtoolize: command not
  found``** — you're running ``./autogen.sh`` on macOS.  Use ``glibtoolize``
  instead, which is what Homebrew's GNU libtool installs as.  Either alias
  it in your shell (``alias libtoolize=glibtoolize``) or edit the autogen
  script to call ``glibtoolize`` directly.

* **``configure`` silently skips the ``-python`` command** — ``python3``
  on your ``PATH`` doesn't match the one the configure script probed, or
  the NumPy headers aren't installed for it.  Check which interpreter
  ``configure`` found by examining the output.  Homebrew's ``python@3`` is
  safest for this because ``brew install numpy`` sets it up correctly.

* **``-parallel N`` produced errors on older macOS** — this was fixed in
  VARTOOLS 1.51 (the code now uses named semaphores on macOS since ``sem_init``
  is unsupported).  Upgrade to 1.51 or newer.

* **Runtime error ``image not found`` for a Homebrew library** — after
  upgrading Homebrew, library versions may have changed.  Rebuild
  (``make clean && make``) after significant Homebrew upgrades.
